#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gcs-testscan -- scan the checktree and build runner lists.

Replaces the detour through `check.exp dump_test_list`, which writes five
Groovy files of Jenkins Job DSL **into the testsuite git tree** (tracked, not
ignored -- one run produced 3783 changed lines here).

What is scanned is what the check.exp files declare:

    set check_name           "<name>"
    set check_category       "... VERIFIED ..."
    set check_needs          "init_core_system display_test"
    set check_description(N) "..."        -> level N is supported
    lappend check_functions  "..."        -> test has any test functions at all

Selection as dump_test_list does it (check.exp:8870ff): absolute path, has
check_functions, has check_category, not JENKINS_DISABLED, must be VERIFIED --
with ONE deliberate difference, see "Non-leaf checks" below.

Non-leaf checks
---------------
dump_test_list drops every node that has children ("skipped ... because it has
children", check.exp:8893). That rule is wrong, and this scanner used to copy
it. A directory can hold a check of its own AND contain further checks below it
-- the framework says so itself where it aggregates the runlevels:

    # add the levels of its children (and a directory can both contain a check
    # and have subdirectories)                              check.exp:5734

Eleven directories in the 92x checktree are exactly that. Seven of them are
VERIFIED, enabled and had no ctest entry at all, so a "green full run" never
touched them -- among them system_tests/clients/qconf with its 61 check
functions, which is where qconf_de_check lives, the check that found the
`qconf -de` deadlock of CS-2438. The Jenkins side has the same blind spot,
because it is generated from the same rule (CS-2520).

The correct predicate is not "is a leaf" but "declares check_functions", and
that is tested three lines further down anyway: a pure container node has no
check_functions and drops out on its own. So the leaf test is simply gone.

One of the eleven is a container only by accident: qsub_override_requests has a
single subdirectory named test_files, a data directory with no check.exp at all.
read_checktree_nodes (check.exp:3121) descends into every subdirectory except
tcl_files and makes each one a child, so a folder of input files is enough to
remove a check from the run. That is also why moving the parent check.exp down
into a leaf of its own would not have been a general fix.

Dependencies
------------
`validate_needs` (check.exp:3227) checks whether a result file exists in
$CHECK_RESULT_DIRS(completed|unsupported) for each entry of check_needs. That
directory is separate **per runner** -- a dependency satisfied on r1 is
invisible to r2. Hence:

  * Infrastructure tests (init_core_system, display_test, connection_test, ...)
    are a prerequisite on EVERY runner and run there first.
  * Anything coupled beyond that forms a group that goes to ONE runner as a
    whole -- including mutual dependencies (save_config needs load_config and
    vice versa), which are not a DAG and cannot be resolved topologically.

Distribution is by measured runtime (LPT, longest group first) from
results/check_durations.txt.

Bundling
--------
Every invocation of check.exp pays for itself before it runs a test: startup,
the enhanced setup with the translations, and the ssh sessions to the hosts add
up to about 25 s. For a unit whose own work is twenty seconds that is more than
the test. So groups shorter than GCS_BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT are merged into bundles of
at most GCS_BUNDLE_BUDGET seconds and handed to gcs-run-unit as one invocation,
which runs them one after another in a single testsuite.

A bundle is one ctest entry named "<first unit>+group", so a failure anywhere
in it marks the entry. That is why `split` writes each bundled unit a second
time as its own entry labelled `single`:

  ctest -LE single      what a run works through -- bundles and long units
  ctest -L  single      the individual entries, for repeating single tests

gcs-testrun uses exactly that split: phase 1 excludes the label, phases 2 and 3
select by test name and therefore land on the single entries. Nobody repeats a
bundle because one test in it failed.

Usage:
  gcs-testscan <line> list                     # all units as TSV
  gcs-testscan <line> groups                   # dependency groups
  gcs-testscan <line> split <N> [--out DIR]    # N runner lists + ctest file
  gcs-testscan <line> verify                   # check against dump_test_list
  gcs-testscan <line> diff                     # what changed against the last split
  gcs-testscan <line> ctest-names              # the registered names, one per line
  gcs-testscan <line> clion-configs <DIR>      # IDE run configurations

Environment:
  GCS_BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT   120   longest group that may go into a bundle, seconds
  GCS_BUNDLE_BUDGET     600   size of a bundle, seconds; 0 switches bundling off
  GCS_TS_OVERHEAD        48   cost of one invocation, used for the estimate
  GCS_CLION_ROOT              where the lines live, default ~/Clion
"""

import os, re, sys, glob, collections

CLION_ROOT = os.environ.get("GCS_CLION_ROOT", os.path.expanduser("~/Clion"))

_SEG_CACHE = {}


def segment(sub, tree):
    """Ask gcs-segment where <sub> belongs for <tree> in this lab segment.

    gcs-segment -- the sibling tool gcs-pipeline, gcs-runners and gcs-teststate
    ask as well -- is the single source of truth for the A/B distinction, so this
    stays correct on both sides. Falls back to the in-tree location, which is
    exactly segment A's answer: a missing helper degrades to what A has always
    done rather than to nothing. Cached, the answer cannot change while this
    process lives.
    """
    key = (sub, tree)
    if key in _SEG_CACHE:
        return _SEG_CACHE[key]
    d = tree if sub == "map" else f"{tree}/{sub}"
    import subprocess
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    try:
        out = subprocess.run([f"{here}/gcs-segment", sub, tree],
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             universal_newlines=True, timeout=5)
        if out.stdout.strip():
            d = out.stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        pass
    _SEG_CACHE[key] = d
    return d


def measure_roots():
    """The roots under which measurements are looked for.

    One on segment A. Two on B, because the fleet and the lines' own results sit
    on the /scratch export while the trees stay in the home -- and a tree part
    way through the move has files under both. Duplicates cost nothing here: the
    longest run wins regardless of which file it came from.
    """
    return list(dict.fromkeys([CLION_ROOT, segment("map", CLION_ROOT)]))

# Tests that are a prerequisite for others without representing a functional
# coupling of their own: they have to run once up front on every runner.
INFRA = {"init_core_system", "connection_test", "display_test"}

# Checks that are not scheduled as a unit of their own.
#
# init_core_system (install_core_system/) installs the cluster. Every runner
# already runs it as the pipeline's `install` action before the run starts --
# that is what puts its result file into the runner's results/ and satisfies the
# check_needs of everything else. Scheduling it a second time would reinstall a
# slot's cluster in the middle of the run for no gain.
#
# It is listed here because until CS-2520 it was excluded by accident: it has two
# subdirectories and therefore fell out with the leaf rule. Now that non-leaf
# checks are scanned, the exclusion has to be stated.
#
# connection_test and display_test are deliberately NOT here. They are in INFRA
# as well, but only so their check_needs edges are ignored when groups are
# formed; as units they have always run, and they test something (the ssh and
# X11 reachability of the host set) that nothing else covers.
NOT_A_UNIT = {"init_core_system"}

# Cost per check.exp invocation (setup + teardown, without the test body).
#
# A single measurement on an idle machine gives 25 s, but under eight parallel
# invocations it is a median of 48 s and a mean of 61 s (494 units of the full
# run on 2026-07-31, wall clock minus the duration the testsuite reports). The
# setup opens ssh connections to all hosts; eight of those at once against the
# same six VMs cost more than one. The test bodies themselves do not get slower
# -- that was measured separately (8 in parallel: 122 s against 124 s alone).
#
# With 25 s the model predicted 2.33 h where it actually took 3.13 h. The median
# is the more robust value, because a few outliers up to 473 s skew the mean.
OVERHEAD = int(os.environ.get("GCS_TS_OVERHEAD", "48"))

# Tests that cannot share the host set with the rest of the run.
#
# ts_cluster gives every test its own cluster, which is enough for anything whose
# behaviour is local to that cluster. These are not: they measure or occupy
# something the whole host set shares, so a second one running anywhere makes both
# meaningless.
#
#   display    every runner uses the same DISPLAY (ebablick,envlist -> h047:10).
#              An X11 test that grabs it while another is using it fails for a
#              reason that has nothing to do with the product.
#
# NOT here, and deliberately so: the user's .sge_request and .sge_aliases, which
# path_alias and file_parsing write. They looked like the same kind of resource --
# one file, shared by every cluster of the operating system user -- but a token
# would only have held the two writers apart. The other 31 runners *read* those
# files on every submit, so whoever held the token still changed how they
# behaved. There is no exclusivity that fixes a shared read. CS-707 gave the
# clients SGE_TEST_USER_HOME_DIR instead, and the testsuite hands each runner a
# directory of its own, so nothing is shared and nothing needs holding apart.
#
# A scarce resource is preferable to RUN_SERIAL: it holds apart only what
# conflicts, and the other 31 runners keep working meanwhile.
#
# The cost floor exists because COST is taken from recorded durations, and those
# come from runs where the test was NOT competing. throughput.9 was costed 170 s
# and took 1540 s; ctest starts in descending COST order, so it began 58 % into
# the run and then held phase 1 open alone for 25 minutes. A floor puts it back
# at the front where its true length belongs.
# The floor exists so the serialised chain STARTS early -- ctest orders by
# descending COST, and a chain that begins late cannot finish before the parallel
# work does. It is not a prediction of the duration.
#
# NOT here, deliberately: performance/throughput. It produced five artifacts of
# the 2026-08-04 run and the reasoning applies to it -- but the family has 48
# units in phase 1, and once they are kept out of bundles (as a resource holder
# must be) serialising them is a chain of ~4 h against a run of 1.4 h. It would
# also still compete with the other 27 clusters, so it would not even buy a quiet
# host set. Throughput needs a different answer: its own pass at -j 1, or a test
# that tolerates incomplete accounting records instead of failing on them.
SHARED_RESOURCES = ("display",)
EXCLUSIVE = (
    ("display", re.compile(r"/qrsh_ijs_(x11_|reconnect_x11)"), 600),
    # path_alias submits through qsh and therefore needs the display as well.
    ("display", re.compile(r"/system_tests/config/path_alias$"), 300),
)


def exclusive_for(units):
    """Extra resource tokens and the cost floor for a group of units.

    A bundle needs the resource as soon as ONE of its units does -- the units of
    a bundle run one after another in the same invocation, so the bundle holds
    the resource for its whole runtime.
    """
    tokens, floor = [], 0
    for name, rx, fl in EXCLUSIVE:
        if any(rx.search(u["path"]) for u in units):
            # A resource may be claimed by more than one entry -- display is, for
            # the x11 tests and for path_alias. ctest reads "display:1,display:1"
            # as a demand for two of a resource that has one slot, and the test
            # would never be scheduled.
            if f"{name}:1" not in tokens:
                tokens.append(f"{name}:1")
            floor = max(floor, fl)
    return tokens, floor


def wait_factor():
    """The factor gcs-run-unit hands to the testsuite as timeout_factor.

    Read here from the same variable with the same default, because the two
    numbers must not drift apart. The ctest timeout is a ceiling on a whole
    unit; the factor raises how long a test may spend inside its wait helpers
    before it gives up. Scale one without the other and ctest kills the test
    before it can report why it failed -- which is what happened to
    backup_restore.0 in the run of 2026-08-02: killed at the 900 s floor in both
    phases, two of its waits at 180 s instead of 60, and the actual defect (the
    execds do not come back after the restore) only visible in the log.

    Only the floor is scaled. cost * 3 already leaves a long test room to spare,
    and the floor is what short tests get -- exactly the ones whose failure path
    is longer than their normal run.
    """
    try:
        f = float(os.environ.get("CS_TIMEOUT_FACTOR", "3"))
    except ValueError:
        return 1
    return int(f) if f >= 1 else 1


def read_sav(path, key):
    """Last occurrence wins -- a .sav carries the previous generation before it."""
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return ""
    want, last, take = f":{key}:", "", False
    for line in open(path, errors="replace"):
        line = line.rstrip("\n")
        if take:
            last, take = line.strip(":"), False
        elif line == want:
            take = True
    return last


def checktree_roots(line):
    cfg = f"{CLION_ROOT}/{line}/config/defaults-{line}.sav"
    roots = [read_sav(cfg, "checktree_root_dir")]
    extra = read_sav(cfg, "additional_checktree_dirs")
    if extra and extra != "none":
        roots += extra.split()
    return [r for r in roots if r and os.path.isdir(r)]


def enabled_runlevels(line):
    """The runlevel bands the framework actually executes.

    is_level_enabled (check.exp:3748) accepts a level when it falls into
    [start, start+99] for one of the starts in CHECK_ENABLED_RUNLEVELS, and
    enhanced_setup pins that list to "0" -- the short band. The other bands
    (medium, long, day, week) are commented out beside it.

    A level outside the enabled bands is not skipped quietly. run_tests returns
    2, the invocation logs "test does not support actual runlevel" and exits
    *ok* with zero tests run -- after paying a full check.exp startup, ~48 s.
    Registering such a unit therefore buys no coverage and hides that fact
    behind a green result. Measured on the 92x checktree: 48 units, about 38
    minutes of a parallel run.

    Read out of check.exp rather than duplicated here, so that enabling a band
    there is all it takes for the units to appear.

    Taken from enhanced_setup specifically, not from the last assignment in the
    file -- file order is not execution order. The variable is assigned in eight
    places: the file scope default, enhanced_setup, the interactive runlevel
    menu, and several that clear it temporarily. One of those, the "all
    <runlevel>" action, rebuilds it as 0..n*100 and thereby widens the bands --
    but that is the fullrun path. What gcs-run-unit drives is "check <path>
    <level>", and there the value from enhanced_setup is what holds.
    """
    for root in checktree_roots(line):
        path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(root), "check.exp")
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        txt = open(path, errors="replace").read()
        body = re.search(r'^proc\s+enhanced_setup\s*\{.*?^\}', txt, re.M | re.S)
        for where in (body.group(0) if body else "", txt):
            found = re.findall(r'^\s*set\s+CHECK_ENABLED_RUNLEVELS\s+"([^"]*)"',
                               where, re.M)
            # the first non-empty one: the clearing assignments carry ""
            for value in found:
                if value.split():
                    return [int(x) for x in value.split()]
    return [0]


RE = {
    "name":     re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_name\s+"([^"]*)"', re.M),
    "category": re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_category\s+"([^"]*)"', re.M),
    "needs":    re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_needs\s+"([^"]*)"', re.M),
    "desc":     re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_description\((\d+)\)', re.M),
    "func":     re.compile(r'^\s*(?:lappend|set)\s+check_functions\s+"', re.M),
    # Both spellings occur: {"9.1.0" "9.2.0"} and "9.0.5 9.2.0"
    "vr_all":   re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_version_range\s+(?:\{([^}]*)\}|"([^"]*)")', re.M),
    "vr_lvl":   re.compile(r'^\s*set\s+check_version_range\((\d+)\)\s+(?:\{([^}]*)\}|"([^"]*)")', re.M),
}


def _ver(s):
    """'9.2.0prealpha' -> (9,2,0). Trailing non-digits are ignored."""
    parts = []
    for p in s.split(".")[:3]:
        m = re.match(r"\d+", p)
        parts.append(int(m.group()) if m else 0)
    while len(parts) < 3:
        parts.append(0)
    return tuple(parts)


def in_range(current, spec):
    """from inclusive, to exclusive -- as is_version_in_range (version.tcl:369).

    That is why {"9.1.0" "9.2.0"} excludes the 9.2 line.
    """
    vs = re.findall(r'"([^"]*)"', spec) or spec.split()
    if not vs:
        return True
    if current < _ver(vs[0]):
        return False
    if len(vs) > 1 and vs[1] and current >= _ver(vs[1]):
        return False
    return True


def parse(path, current):
    try:
        txt = open(os.path.join(path, "check.exp"), errors="replace").read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    m = RE["name"].search(txt)
    if not m:
        return None

    levels = sorted({int(x) for x in RE["desc"].findall(txt)})

    # Version range: either global for the whole test, or per runlevel as an
    # array (verify_version_range, check.exp:2964).
    per_lvl = {int(l): (a or b) for l, a, b in RE["vr_lvl"].findall(txt)}
    if per_lvl:
        levels = [l for l in levels if l not in per_lvl or in_range(current, per_lvl[l])]
    else:
        g = RE["vr_all"].search(txt)
        if g and not in_range(current, g.group(1) or g.group(2)):
            levels = []

    cat = RE["category"].search(txt)
    needs = RE["needs"].search(txt)
    return {
        "path":     path,
        "name":     m.group(1),
        "category": cat.group(1) if cat else "",
        "needs":    needs.group(1).split() if needs else [],
        "levels":   levels,
        "has_func": bool(RE["func"].search(txt)),
    }


def current_version(line):
    """Derive the product version (9,2,0) from gridengine_version ('92')."""
    v = read_sav(f"{CLION_ROOT}/{line}/config/defaults-{line}.sav", "gridengine_version")
    if len(v) == 2 and v.isdigit():
        return (int(v[0]), int(v[1]), 0)
    return _ver(v) if v else (9, 2, 0)


def scan(line):
    """All tests of the checktrees -- see "Non-leaf checks" in the header.

    Deliberately NOT restricted to leaf directories: a directory can carry a
    check of its own and still have checks below it, and dropping those is what
    kept seven VERIFIED checks out of every parallel run (CS-2520).
    """
    current = current_version(line)
    bands = enabled_runlevels(line)
    tests = []
    for root in checktree_roots(line):
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
            if "check.exp" not in filenames:
                continue
            t = parse(dirpath, current)
            if not t:
                continue
            # --- selection as in dump_test_list (check.exp:8870ff) ---
            # A container node has no check_functions and drops out here. This
            # is the test that the removed leaf rule was a bad proxy for.
            if not t["has_func"]:
                continue
            if t["name"] in NOT_A_UNIT:
                continue
            if not t["category"]:
                continue
            if "JENKINS_DISABLED" in t["category"]:
                continue
            if "VERIFIED" not in t["category"]:
                continue
            # Only levels of an enabled band -- see enabled_runlevels().
            t["levels"] = [lv for lv in t["levels"]
                           if any(s <= lv <= s + 99 for s in bands)]
            if not t["levels"]:
                continue
            tests.append(t)
    return sorted(tests, key=lambda t: t["path"])


def root_spellings():
    """Every spelling of CLION_ROOT a stored path can carry.

    Three of them, and they are not interchangeable as strings. The paths in
    check_durations.txt come from the testsuite, which is configured from the
    .sav files and therefore says /home/<user>/... . CLION_ROOT is built from
    $HOME instead, and on the server that expands to /export/home/<user>/... .
    And on segment B the same tree is reachable a third time under /scratch.

    Matching one spelling against another never succeeds, which is not a
    cosmetic failure: rel_key() then fell through to the full path, every line
    kept keys of its own, and the cross-line sharing this exists for was lost
    without any sign of it.
    """
    out = [CLION_ROOT]
    if CLION_ROOT.startswith("/export/home/"):
        out.append("/home" + CLION_ROOT[len("/export/home"):])
    elif CLION_ROOT.startswith("/home/"):
        out.append("/export/home" + CLION_ROOT[len("/home"):])
    out += [segment("map", r) for r in list(out)]
    return list(dict.fromkeys(out))


_REL_RE = None


def rel_key(path):
    """Line independent key: strip '<root>/<line>/' or
    '<root>/<line>-features/<name>/' off the path.

    check_durations.txt keys on absolute paths that contain the line. A freshly
    set up 92x would therefore find no runtime at all -- even though measured
    values have long existed for the same test under 91x, 90x or gcs0. The same
    test has the same path *below* the line under every line.
    """
    global _REL_RE
    if _REL_RE is None:
        _REL_RE = [re.compile(re.escape(r) + r"/(?:[^/]+-features/[^/]+|[^/]+)/(.*)$")
                   for r in root_spellings()]
    for rx in _REL_RE:
        m = rx.match(path)
        if m:
            return m.group(1)
    return path


def durations(line):
    """path,level -> seconds from check_durations.txt (the LONGEST run wins).

    Not the last one: the file of the line and the file of every runner hold the
    same test with quite different times -- upgrade_config was found with 518,
    1365, 1407 and 1536 s -- and which file is read last is an accident of the
    glob order.

    The longest is the right choice for LPT. Underestimating the longest job is
    what costs makespan: it gets scheduled late and becomes the tail everyone
    waits for. Overestimating only moves it forward, and finishing early hurts
    nobody. Measured: the installer group was planned with 1438 s and took 1875
    to 2121 s, so it started fourth instead of first.
    """
    out = {}
    # The runners' own files belong in here. They are where a parallel run
    # writes its measurements, and without them the model is fed only by the
    # lines' serial runs -- everything that has only ever run on a runner stays
    # "estimated" forever. That is not academic: upgrade_config levels 1 and 2
    # were planned with the 32 s default although 1441 s and 200 s had been
    # measured on r12, and level 1 was then killed by ctest at the timeout
    # derived from those 32 s (run of 2026-08-02, phase 1).
    files = []
    for _r in measure_roots():
        files += glob.glob(f"{_r}/*/results/check_durations.txt")
        files += glob.glob(f"{_r}/*/runners/r*/results/check_durations.txt")
        files += glob.glob(f"{_r}/*-features/*/results/check_durations.txt")
        files += glob.glob(f"{_r}/*-features/*/runners/r*/results/check_durations.txt")
    for f in files:
        lines = open(f, errors="replace").read().split("\n")
        for i, l in enumerate(lines):
            if l.startswith(":") and l.endswith(":") and "," in l:
                key = l[1:-1]
                p, _, lv = key.rpartition(",")
                if not lv.isdigit() or i + 1 >= len(lines):
                    continue
                v = lines[i + 1].strip(":")
                if v.isdigit():
                    k = (p, int(lv))
                    out[k] = max(out.get(k, 0), int(v))
    return out


def generic_durations(exact):
    """Median per (line independent path, level) across all lines."""
    buckets = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for (p, lv), v in exact.items():
        if v > 0:
            buckets[(rel_key(p), lv)].append(v)
    return {k: sorted(v)[len(v) // 2] for k, v in buckets.items()}


def units(line):
    """Units = (test, level). Duration from measurement, else the median."""
    ts = scan(line)
    dur = durations(line)
    gen = generic_durations(dur)
    known = [d for d in dur.values() if d > 0]
    fallback = sorted(known)[len(known) // 2] if known else 60
    us = []
    for t in ts:
        for lv in t["levels"]:
            # 1. own measurement, 2. the same location under a different line,
            # 3. median. Only this way does a fresh testsuite have any data.
            if (t["path"], lv) in dur:
                net, src = dur[(t["path"], lv)], "measured"
            elif (rel_key(t["path"]), lv) in gen:
                net, src = gen[(rel_key(t["path"]), lv)], "other_line"
            else:
                net, src = fallback, "estimated"
            us.append({**t, "level": lv, "dur": net + OVERHEAD,
                       "net": net, "src": src, "measured": src != "estimated"})
    return us, fallback


def group(us):
    """Combine units into groups that have to go to the same runner.

    Edges only arise from NON-infrastructure dependencies. The group is the
    connected component (undirected), because cycles such as
    save_config <-> load_config admit no topological order.
    """
    # The element of the union is (test, level), NOT the test name. Otherwise
    # all levels of a test would be forced onto one runner -- for throughput
    # that would be 48 invocations back to back. Levels stand on their own:
    # dump_test_list has always produced one Jenkins job per (test, level), so
    # the CI has long spread them across different clusters.
    by_name = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for u in us:
        by_name[u["name"]].append((u["name"], u["level"]))

    parent = {(u["name"], u["level"]): (u["name"], u["level"]) for u in us}

    def find(a):
        while parent[a] != a:
            parent[a] = parent[parent[a]]
            a = parent[a]
        return a

    def union(a, b):
        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)
        if ra != rb:
            parent[ra] = rb

    # A real check_needs edge binds ALL units of both tests together:
    # validate_needs is satisfied by ANY level of the dependency, so at least one
    # of them must have run on the same runner.
    for u in us:
        for n in u["needs"]:
            if n in INFRA or n not in by_name:
                continue
            for other in by_name[n]:
                union((u["name"], u["level"]), other)

    groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for u in us:
        groups[find((u["name"], u["level"]))].append(u)
    # within the group: levels ascending, so the lower ones run first
    for g in groups.values():
        g.sort(key=lambda u: (u["name"], u["level"]))
    return list(groups.values())


# A bundle is paid for once: startup, the enhanced setup and the ssh sessions to
# the hosts cost about 25 s per invocation, measured on 2026-08-03 (seven tests
# of object/role took 356 s as seven invocations and 201 s as one). For a test
# whose own work is twenty seconds that is more than the test itself.
#
# Only short units are bundled. A long one gains little in relative terms and
# brings the whole bundle's risk with it: a bundle is one ctest entry, so a
# failure anywhere in it marks the entry.
BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT = int(os.environ.get("GCS_BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT", "120"))

# The budget bounds the tail, not the saving. At 32 runners the makespan is set
# by the total work divided by the runners -- about 3200 s -- and the longest
# single unit is 1457 s. A 600 s bundle stays clear of both and still holds ten
# units of a minute each. GCS_BUNDLE_BUDGET=0 switches bundling off entirely.
BUNDLE_BUDGET = int(os.environ.get("GCS_BUNDLE_BUDGET", "600"))


def bundle(groups):
    """Merge short groups into bundles that stay within BUNDLE_BUDGET.

    Groups, not units: what group() tied together because of check_needs has to
    run on one runner, and a bundle keeps that because it is scheduled as a
    whole.

    Longest first, then first fit -- not optimal, but it keeps the larger ones
    from being spread over bundles that all end up at the budget.
    """
    if BUNDLE_BUDGET <= 0:
        return groups

    def dur(g):
        return sum(u["dur"] for u in g)

    # A group that needs a scarce resource never goes into a bundle: the bundle
    # would hold the resource for its whole runtime, including the unrelated
    # tests packed next to it, and those would then be serialised for no reason.
    def locks(g):
        return bool(exclusive_for(g)[0])

    small = sorted([g for g in groups if dur(g) <= BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT and not locks(g)],
                   key=lambda g: -dur(g))
    out = [g for g in groups if dur(g) > BUNDLE_MAX_UNIT or locks(g)]

    packed = []
    for g in small:
        for b in packed:
            if sum(dur(x) for x in b) + dur(g) <= BUNDLE_BUDGET:
                b.append(g)
                break
        else:
            packed.append([g])
    for b in packed:
        merged = [u for g in b for u in g]
        # The invocation is paid once for the whole bundle, so only the first
        # unit carries the overhead and the rest count with their net time. Left
        # unadjusted the model would plan against a cost that bundling has just
        # removed -- and pack worse for it, which is exactly what the first
        # attempt showed: makespan 3349 s against 3289 s unbundled.
        for u in merged[1:]:
            u["dur"] = u["net"]
        out.append(merged)
    return out


def split(us, n):
    """LPT: heaviest group first, onto the emptiest runner at the time."""
    gs = sorted(bundle(group(us)), key=lambda g: -sum(u["dur"] for u in g))
    bins = [[] for _ in range(n)]
    load = [0] * n
    for g in gs:
        i = load.index(min(load))
        bins[i].extend(g)
        load[i] += sum(u["dur"] for u in g)
    return bins, load


def write_ctest(line, outdir, us, n):
    """Generate CTestTestfile.cmake and the resource specification.

    One add_test definition per *group*, not per unit: ctest assigns to free
    slots dynamically and could therefore put save_config and load_config on
    different clusters -- and their dependency runs through the runner's own
    results/ directory, so it would be broken. As a single ctest definition the
    group stays together and in order.

    RESOURCE_GROUPS is the ctest equivalent of Jenkins' lockableResources:
    ctest guarantees exclusivity and tells the test through
    CTEST_RESOURCE_GROUP_0_TS_CLUSTER which slot it was given. COST makes ctest
    start in descending order of cost under -j, so the LPT ordering comes for
    free.
    """
    os.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)
    runner = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "gcs-run-unit")

    with open(f"{outdir}/ts_cluster.json", "w") as fh:
        fh.write('{ "version": {"major": 1, "minor": 0},\n  "local": [ { "ts_cluster": [\n')
        fh.write(",\n".join(f'    {{"id": "{i}", "slots": 1}}' for i in range(1, n + 1)))
        fh.write('\n  ],\n')
        # One slot each: these are not per-cluster resources but properties of the
        # whole host set, so exactly one test may hold them at a time.
        for extra in SHARED_RESOURCES:
            fh.write(f'  "{extra}": [ {{"id": "1", "slots": 1}} ],\n')
        fh.seek(fh.tell() - 2); fh.write("\n  } ] }\n")

    def band(lv):
        return ["short", "medium", "long", "day", "week"][min(lv // 100, 4)]

    with open(f"{outdir}/CTestTestfile.cmake", "w") as fh:
        fh.write(f"# generated by gcs-testscan for line {line} -- do not edit by hand\n")
        for g in sorted(bundle(group(us)), key=lambda g: -sum(u["dur"] for u in g)):
            first = g[0]
            # The level belongs in the name: without it the levels of the same
            # test collide into one definition and ctest would see 452 instead
            # of 580 tests. Groups carry the name of their first test.
            tname = rel_key(first["path"]).replace("/", ".") + f".{first['level']}"
            if len({u["name"] for u in g}) > 1:
                tname += "+group"
            args = " ".join(f'"{u["path"]}" "{u["level"]}"' for u in g)
            cost = sum(u["dur"] for u in g)
            res_tokens, cost_floor = exclusive_for(g)
            cost = max(cost, cost_floor)
            groups = ",".join(["ts_cluster:1"] + res_tokens)
            labels = sorted({w for u in g for w in u["category"].split()} |
                            {band(u["level"]) for u in g})
            # --name before the line: it is an option, and gcs-run-unit reads
            # its options first. ctest exposes no variable carrying the test
            # name, so without passing it the execution unit cannot record its
            # result under the name the rest of the tooling uses.
            fh.write(f'add_test("{tname}" "{runner}" "--name" "{tname}" "{line}" {args})\n')
            fh.write(f'set_tests_properties("{tname}" PROPERTIES\n')
            fh.write(f'  RESOURCE_GROUPS "{groups}"\n')
            fh.write(f'  COST {cost}\n')
            fh.write(f'  TIMEOUT {max(900 * wait_factor(), cost * 3)}\n')
            fh.write(f'  LABELS "{";".join(labels)}")\n')

            # A second definition for every unit of a bundle, so a rerun can
            # take the test that failed instead of the bundle around it. Phase 1
            # skips them with -LE single; phases 2 and 3 select them by name from
            # the ledger, which since 2026-08-03 records one entry per test even
            # when they ran together.
            #
            # Without this a single failure costs the whole bundle twice over --
            # up to 600 s in phase 2 and again in phase 3 -- which would eat a
            # good part of what the bundling just saved.
            if len(g) > 1:
                for u in g:
                    sname = ctest_name(u)
                    sres, sfloor = exclusive_for([u])
                    scost = max(u["net"] + OVERHEAD, sfloor)
                    sgroups = ",".join(["ts_cluster:1"] + sres)
                    slabels = sorted(set(u["category"].split()) | {band(u["level"]), "single"})
                    fh.write(f'add_test("{sname}" "{runner}" "--name" "{sname}" '
                             f'"{line}" "{u["path"]}" "{u["level"]}")\n')
                    fh.write(f'set_tests_properties("{sname}" PROPERTIES\n')
                    fh.write(f'  RESOURCE_GROUPS "{sgroups}"\n')
                    fh.write(f'  COST {scost}\n')
                    fh.write(f'  TIMEOUT {max(900 * wait_factor(), scost * 3)}\n')
                    fh.write(f'  LABELS "{";".join(slabels)}")\n')

    print(f"  ctest definition:   {outdir}/CTestTestfile.cmake")
    print(f"  wait factor:        {wait_factor()}  (floor of the ctest timeout: "
          f"{900 * wait_factor()} s)")
    print(f"  resource spec:      {outdir}/ts_cluster.json  ({n} slots)")
    print(f"  run with:  ctest --test-dir {outdir} -j {n} "
          f"--resource-spec-file {outdir}/ts_cluster.json")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CLion configs

# Naming scheme (the axis comes first so the IDE's search narrows on it):
#
#   ts dir  ts/system_tests/scheduler   [26]
#   ts cat  SCHEDULER                   [94]
#   ts      ts/testsuite/connection_test.0
#
# The technical prefixes (testsuite.src.checktree. and
# ocs-testsuite.checktree_gcs.checktree.) are dropped -- they carry no
# information and would make every entry start with the same characters, which
# is exactly what defeats a search box. What is left of them is one character of
# provenance: "ts" for the classic checktree, "gcs" for ocs-testsuite.
#
# Slashes for the path, a dot only before the level, so a path reads as a path.
# The count in brackets is there because the difference between 26 and 153 tests
# is the difference between ten minutes and half a day -- worth knowing before
# clicking.

CONFIG_XML = """\
<component name="ProjectRunConfigurationManager">
  <configuration default="false" name="{name}" type="CTestRunConfiguration" \
factoryName="CTestRun" PROGRAM_PARAMS="{params}" REDIRECT_INPUT="false" \
ELEVATE="false" USE_EXTERNAL_CONSOLE="false" EMULATE_TERMINAL="false" \
WORKING_DIR="file://$CMakeCurrentLocalGenerationDir$" PASS_PARENT_ENVS_2="true" \
CONFIG_NAME="{profile}" RUN_PATH="$CTestCurrentExecutableName$" \
EXPLICIT_BUILD_TARGET_NAME="all" TEST_MODE="SUITE_TEST">
    <method v="2">
      <option name="com.jetbrains.cidr.execution.CidrBuildBeforeRunTaskProvider$BuildBeforeRunTask" enabled="true" />
      <option name="BeforeTestRunTask" enabled="true" />
    </method>
  </configuration>
</component>
"""


def ctest_name(u):
    """The name write_ctest() gives this unit."""
    return rel_key(u["path"]).replace("/", ".") + f".{u['level']}"


def pretty(u):
    """ts/system_tests/clients/qmod/qmod_general.0"""
    rel = rel_key(u["path"])
    tree = "gcs" if rel.startswith("ocs-testsuite") else "ts"
    parts = rel.split("/")
    cut = parts.index("checktree") + 1 if "checktree" in parts else 0
    return f"{tree}/" + "/".join(parts[cut:]) + f".{u['level']}"


def write_clion_configs(line, outdir, us, jobs, profile, min_label, with_tests):
    import xml.sax.saxutils as sx
    os.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)
    # Remove what a previous run wrote, so a renamed or deleted test does not
    # leave an entry behind that no longer selects anything.
    for f in glob.glob(f"{outdir}/gcs_ts_*.xml"):
        os.remove(f)

    def esc(t):
        return re.sub(r"([.^$*+?()\[\]{}|\\])", r"\\\1", t)

    def emit(kind, name, params):
        fn = re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]+", "_", f"{kind}_{name}").strip("_")
        with open(f"{outdir}/gcs_ts_{fn}.xml", "w") as fh:
            fh.write(CONFIG_XML.format(name=sx.quoteattr(name)[1:-1],
                                       params=sx.quoteattr(params)[1:-1],
                                       profile=profile))

    # Work on the groups, not the units: a dependency group is ONE ctest test
    # (four units of the installer group are a single "...inst_sge_upd.0+group"
    # definition). Counting units would promise four tests where ctest offers
    # one, and the number in the name is only useful if it is the number that
    # will actually run.
    groups = sorted(group(us), key=lambda g: g[0]["path"])
    regs = []
    for g in groups:
        first = g[0]
        name = ctest_name(first) + ("+group" if len({u["name"] for u in g}) > 1 else "")
        regs.append((name, first, g))

    # Two configurations that select nothing in particular. "everything" is the
    # one to be on when re-running a single test from the results tree: an IDE
    # passes the position of a test there, and a position only means the same
    # thing to ctest when no name filter has reduced and renumbered the set.
    emit("a", "all     everything", f"-V -j {jobs}")
    # --rerun-failed selects by name (LastTestsFailed.log holds names), so it
    # composes with anything and works out of any results tree.
    emit("a", "all     rerun failed", f"-V -j {jobs} --rerun-failed")
    # Kept apart on purpose: the testsuite and the C++ module tests are
    # different kinds of test -- minutes against milliseconds, a cluster against
    # a binary. Mixing them in one entry means every run of the quick ones waits
    # for the slow ones.
    emit("a", f"ts all  testsuite  [{len(regs)}]",
         f"-V -j {jobs} -R ^(testsuite|ocs-testsuite)\\.")
    emit("a", "unit    C++ module tests", "-V -R ^test_")
    # What has not passed under the current build. The file is kept current by
    # gcs-teststate on every recorded result, so this needs no preparation step
    # -- which an IDE could not be taught anyway.
    emit("a", "ts all  pending (not yet passed)",
         f"-V -j {jobs} --tests-from-file "
         f"{segment('runners', f'{CLION_ROOT}/{line}')}/pending.txt")
    n = 5

    if with_tests:
        for name, first, g in regs:
            label = pretty(first) + ("  (+group)" if name.endswith("+group") else "")
            emit("t", f"ts      {label}", f"-V -R {esc(name)}")
            n += 1

    dirs = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for name, first, g in regs:
        dirs[pretty(first).rsplit("/", 1)[0]].append(first)
    for d, members in sorted(dirs.items()):
        pfx = ctest_name(members[0]).rsplit(".", 2)[0]
        # The filter is a prefix and therefore covers subdirectories too -- which
        # is what one wants from "run everything under here". So count the whole
        # subtree, not just the direct children, or the number in the name would
        # promise less than the run delivers.
        c = sum(1 for name, _, _ in regs if name.startswith(pfx + "."))
        emit("d", f"ts dir  {d}  [{c}]", f"-V -j {jobs} -R ^{esc(pfx)}\\.")
        n += 1

    labels = collections.Counter()
    for name, first, g in regs:
        for l in {w for u in g for w in u["category"].split()}:
            labels[l] += 1
    # The all-encompassing ones are noise: they mean "everything" and there is
    # already a configuration for that.
    for l in ("VERIFIED", "short", "medium", "long", "day", "week"):
        labels.pop(l, None)
    for l, c in sorted(labels.items()):
        if c >= min_label:
            emit("c", f"ts cat  {l}  [{c}]", f"-V -j {jobs} -L ^{l}$")
            n += 1

    print(f"  {n} run configurations in {outdir}")
    print(f"  (gcs_ts_*.xml -- regenerating replaces them, nothing else is touched)")


def write_snapshot(line, outdir, us, n, load):
    """Lists without provenance are worthless.

    A checkout can add and remove tests -- a list is therefore only ever valid
    for exactly one source state. The manifest records which one that was, so a
    run stays traceable and `diff` can later show what has changed since.
    """
    import subprocess, datetime

    def rev(d):
        try:
            return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d, "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD"],
                                  capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10
                                  ).stdout.strip() or "?"
        except Exception:
            return "?"

    # The registered names, so tools that only need the vocabulary do not have to
    # rescan the checktree. Written here because this is the moment the set is
    # defined.
    with open(f"{outdir}/ctest-names.txt", "w") as fh:
        for g in sorted(group(us), key=lambda g: g[0]["path"]):
            first = g[0]
            fh.write(rel_key(first["path"]).replace("/", ".") + f".{first['level']}"
                     + ("+group" if len({u["name"] for u in g}) > 1 else "") + "\n")

    with open(f"{outdir}/units.tsv", "w") as fh:
        for u in sorted(us, key=lambda u: (u["path"], u["level"])):
            fh.write(f"{u['path']}\t{u['level']}\t{u['net']}\t{u['src']}\n")

    est = sum(1 for u in us if not u["measured"])
    with open(f"{outdir}/manifest.txt", "w") as fh:
        fh.write(f"line          {line}\n")
        fh.write(f"generated     {datetime.datetime.now().isoformat(timespec='seconds')}\n")
        for root in checktree_roots(line):
            repo = root
            while repo != "/" and not os.path.isdir(f"{repo}/.git"):
                repo = os.path.dirname(repo)
            fh.write(f"checktree     {root}  @ {rev(repo)}\n")
        fh.write(f"runners       {n}\n")
        fh.write(f"units         {len(us)}  ({est} without a measured duration)\n")
        fh.write(f"overhead      {OVERHEAD}s per invocation\n")
        fh.write(f"serial        {sum(load)}s\n")
        fh.write(f"makespan      {max(load)}s\n")


def main():
    if len(sys.argv) < 3:
        sys.exit(__doc__)
    line, cmd = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
    us, fallback = units(line)

    if cmd == "list":
        for u in us:
            print(f"{u['path']}\t{u['level']}\t{u['dur']}\t{u['src']}\t{u['name']}")

    elif cmd == "groups":
        gs = sorted(bundle(group(us)), key=lambda g: -sum(u["dur"] for u in g))
        multi = [g for g in gs if len({u["name"] for u in g}) > 1]
        print(f"units: {len(us)}   groups: {len(gs)}   "
              f"of those with more than one test: {len(multi)}")
        for g in multi:
            names = sorted({u["name"] for u in g})
            print(f"  {sum(u['dur'] for u in g):6d}s  {len(g):3d} units  "
                  f"{', '.join(names)}")

    elif cmd == "split":
        n = int(sys.argv[3])
        # Restricting the registered set at generation time, rather than
        # filtering at ctest invocation time, is what keeps an IDE usable:
        # ctest renumbers whatever survives -R or -L, while the IDE takes the
        # index of a test from the full list. Both together find nothing -- or
        # worse, silently the wrong test. If only the wanted tests are
        # registered in the first place, there is nothing to renumber.
        if "--filter" in sys.argv:
            pat = re.compile(sys.argv[sys.argv.index("--filter") + 1])
            keep = [u for u in us if pat.search(rel_key(u["path"]).replace("/", "."))]
            print(f"  filter: {len(keep)} of {len(us)} units")
            us = keep
            if not us:
                sys.exit("  filter matches nothing -- refusing to write an empty set")
        outdir = None
        if "--out" in sys.argv:
            outdir = sys.argv[sys.argv.index("--out") + 1]
            os.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)
        bins, load = split(us, n)
        total = sum(load)
        for i, (b, l) in enumerate(zip(bins, load), 1):
            print(f"  r{i}: {len(b):4d} units  {l:6d}s  ({l/3600:.2f} h)")
            if outdir:
                with open(f"{outdir}/r{i}.list", "w") as fh:
                    for u in b:
                        fh.write(f"{u['path']} {u['level']}\n")
        print(f"  ---- serial {total}s ({total/3600:.2f} h), "
              f"makespan {max(load)}s ({max(load)/3600:.2f} h), "
              f"speedup {total/max(load):.2f}x")
        src = collections.Counter(u["src"] for u in us)
        print("  runtime sources: " +
              ", ".join(f"{k} {v}" for k, v in sorted(src.items())))
        if src["estimated"] > len(us) // 5:
            print(f"  WARNING: {src['estimated']} of {len(us)} units have no "
                  f"runtime data -- the distribution is at best an "
                  f"equal split by count.\n"
                  f"           The first run will be noticeably lopsided, but "
                  f"calibrates afterwards via check_durations.txt.")
        if outdir:
            write_snapshot(line, outdir, us, n, load)
            write_ctest(line, outdir, us, n)
            print(f"  lists in {outdir}/r*.list, manifest in {outdir}/manifest.txt")

    elif cmd == "ctest-names":
        # The names ctest registers, one per line -- the vocabulary every other
        # tool has to speak when it refers to a test.
        for g in sorted(group(us), key=lambda g: g[0]["path"]):
            first = g[0]
            print(rel_key(first["path"]).replace("/", ".") + f".{first['level']}"
                  + ("+group" if len({u["name"] for u in g}) > 1 else ""))

    elif cmd == "clion-configs":
        outdir = sys.argv[3]
        def opt(name, default):
            return sys.argv[sys.argv.index(name) + 1] if name in sys.argv else default
        write_clion_configs(line, outdir, us,
                            jobs=opt("--jobs", "8"),
                            profile=opt("--profile", "GCS Debug h047"),
                            min_label=int(opt("--min-label", "10")),
                            with_tests="--no-tests" not in sys.argv)

    elif cmd == "diff":
        old = sys.argv[3]
        if os.path.isdir(old):
            old = f"{old}/units.tsv"
        prev = set()
        if os.path.isfile(old):
            for l in open(old):
                f = l.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
                if len(f) >= 2 and f[1].isdigit():
                    prev.add((f[0], int(f[1])))
        now = {(u["path"], u["level"]) for u in us}
        added, gone = sorted(now - prev), sorted(prev - now)
        print(f"  before {len(prev)}   now {len(now)}   "
              f"added {len(added)}   removed {len(gone)}")
        for label, s in (("+ added", added), ("- removed", gone)):
            for p, l in s:
                print(f"  {label}: {p} {l}")

    elif cmd == "verify":
        src = f"{CLION_ROOT}/{line}/testsuite/src"
        ref = set()
        for f in glob.glob(f"{src}/jenkins_generate_*.groovy"):
            for m in re.finditer(r"check (/\S+) (\d+)", open(f, errors="replace").read()):
                ref.add((m.group(1), int(m.group(2))))
        mine = {(u["path"], u["level"]) for u in us}
        # The groovy files are generated into the testsuite git tree and are
        # tracked there, so what sits in the working copy may well be the lab's
        # committed version with its own paths. Comparing against that says
        # nothing -- flag it instead of reporting a nonsensical difference.
        foreign = {p for p, _ in ref if not any(p.startswith(r) for r in checktree_roots(line))}
        if foreign:
            print(f"  {len(foreign)} of the dumped paths do not belong to this line,")
            print(f"  e.g. {sorted(foreign)[0]}")
            print(f"  run '{line} dump' first -- the committed groovy files are stale.")
            return
        print(f"  dump_test_list: {len(ref)}   scanner: {len(mine)}")
        for label, s in (("only in dump", ref - mine), ("only in scanner", mine - ref)):
            if s:
                print(f"  {label}: {len(s)}")
                for p, l in sorted(s)[:10]:
                    print(f"    {p} {l}")
        # Since CS-2520 the two sets are expected to differ, and in exactly one
        # direction: dump_test_list drops every check whose directory has
        # subdirectories, this scanner does not. "only in scanner" listing such
        # paths is the fix working, not a defect. "only in dump" still is one.
        extra = sorted({p for p, _ in mine - ref
                        if any(os.path.isdir(os.path.join(p, d))
                               for d in os.listdir(p) if d != "tcl_files")})
        if extra:
            print(f"  of those {len(extra)} are non-leaf checks that dump_test_list")
            print(f"  drops on purpose (check.exp:8893) -- expected, see CS-2520")
        if ref == mine:
            print("  identical")
    else:
        sys.exit(f"unknown command: {cmd}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
